-- title:
--   Primitivity of a normalised connecting idele at level N
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and let $\Lambda_1$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ which is a maximal order in the sense of the project: it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and is finitely generated over $\mathbb{Z}$, and every such order containing it equals it. Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let $m$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$, where $\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ is the finite adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $\widehat{\Lambda_1}$ for [`Submodule.finiteAdeleBox`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L14) $\Lambda_1$, the additive subgroup generated by the elements $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in\Lambda_1$ and $\alpha$ a finite adele integral at every height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$. Assume $m\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, that $N\cdot m^{-1}\in\widehat{\Lambda_1}$, and that the relative index of the additive subgroup of $\Lambda_1\sqcap$ [`Submodule.conjByFiniteIdele`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L31) $\Lambda_1\,m$ in that of $\Lambda_1$ equals $N$, where `conjByFiniteIdele` is the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ obtained by pulling back, along $x\mapsto x\otimes 1$, the image of $\widehat{\Lambda_1}$ under $y\mapsto m\,y\,m^{-1}$. Then for every prime number $\ell$ one has $\ell^{-1}\cdot m\notin\widehat{\Lambda_1}$.
--
--   Under the stated normalisation $\Lambda_1\cap m\widehat{\Lambda_1}m^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $N$ with $\Lambda_1$ as one of its two maximal orders, and the conclusion says that $m$ is primitive: its local elementary divisors at a prime $\ell$ are $(1,\ell^{v_\ell(N)})$ rather than a proper multiple of the identity. It is the quaternion-side input to the level-$N$ Deuring–Eichler dictionary in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where it is used to show that the kernel of the isogeny realising the lattice cut out by $m$ is cyclic.
